we mentioned it there you went into  number 10 and you spoke to the prime  ministers among others about the state  of the NHS thinking to the picture of  you standing on the gates of Downing  Street how was the meeting  um so we're really pleased that it's  been acknowledged at the highest level  and it's pleased that we recognize  there's a crisis because there was a  crisis in emergency medicine we know uh  and this crisis is much worse you know  every winter we seem to go through this  thing there's another a e crisis but  it's important to recognize that what's  going on at the moment is much worse  than the graphs that you were putting up  beforehand showed that yeah and the  visible markers are ambulances waiting  outside hospitals but that is only a  visible marker of the whole system of  being under stress and what we're seeing  is if you go into the emergency  department you'll see the people being  looked after in inappropriate areas with  long ways to be admitted and if you go  onto the wards they're full and  fundamentally the big problem we've got  is as you you showed that we don't have  enough beds our hospitals are too full  for us to do the jobs that we need them  to do it's interesting because you  mentioned there about how we talk about  winter crisis all the time that this is  different and I have to say you know  I've been doing this job for some years  I've spoken to a lot of people working  in the house service at this time and it  feels quite different now I mean  frankly you know you guys seem quite  frightened is that true yes I mean it's  important that people realize that if  they think they need to seek help they  do seek help and one of the anxieties  that we have about all the frost that's  going on at the moment is that people  who are will sit at home when really  they should be seeking help that's  really important but at the same time  pretty much everyone I spoke to they  will say I've never seen it as bad as  this not even during the height of the  covert pandemic so in a very very  different way yes kovid was an  extraordinary time  and but all sorts of things went over it  this feels worse because it's so there's  so many bit strands to it and it's so  difficult and the long Waits that we've  got are water so dangerous and what  we're so worried about and you I mean I  guess the biggest thing people worry  about is safety about lives right and  you believe that lives are being lost  because of delays it's not just belief  so there's some scientific evidence to  back us up with papers that have been  published in scientific journals  everybody who's looked at this has  always found that when you start getting  long waits in emergency departments you  see an increase in mortality  um so there's reasonable scientific data  to support this but not only that we've  also got separate analyzes where people  have looked at ons data and have  suggested that the increase that we've  seen in excess mortality through this  year and there will be lots of factors  at play it's not one single thing but  there'll be lots of factors it's  entirely plausible and it shows this so  we think that problems accessing urgent  and emergency care whether calling an  ambulance waiting a long time for that  being anxious about going to hospital or  the long way to an emergency departments  will be contributing to about a third or  a quarter of all this excess mortality  and you believe that's 300 500 people a  week yeah I mean it will vary but in a  way actually if the number doesn't  matter no you know it's actually not  what we do about it yeah and we know it  shows that there's a problem that needs  fixing okay and just before we go on to  what we should do because I am very keen  to get your view on that and you know  when you talk to ministers and the  government they talk about covert an  awful lot and they say look this is  because of covid because the pressures  we had during covert because of people  playing you know getting appointments  Etc  is that true or do these problems go  back longer oh the problem is go back  way longer yeah if if you look at  performance indicators where we've been  having problems covert absolutely is yet  an additional thing and it's providing  it's taking a lot of disruption onto  hospitals but the problems that we're  holding this go back 2015 you know we  knew that this was a pun before but if  you go back before 2015 we did not have  problems with ambulance Handover delays  we did not have very often people  spending more than 12 hours in emergency  departments and is that under investment  so it's a variety of things  fundamentally the big problem is that we  just our hospitals are too full and you  put up that graph of not having enough  beds but also we're not using those beds  properly and actually in terms of what  we do with our beds we need to make sure  we're using those beds absolutely it's  efficiently and properly as possible  which I know part of that of course is  social care  um kirstama also talks on the program  today about using more capacity in the  private sector do you think that's a  good idea I think we we're in a state of  crisis at the moment and anything we can  do to reduce occupancy within hospitals  is the right thing to do it feels like  an expensive way to try and get  ourselves out of a pickle and I think  long term we've got to look at how we  actually stop this happening again and  that means actually getting to a  probably a more cost effective way to  try and do this by reforming the way we  do social care in this country and I'm  interested to know your thoughts on what  needs to happen in the short term but  also in the long term what do you think  needs to be done so it the big question  but fundamentally the thing we need to  do is reduce the occupancy in our  hospitals and that means having more  hospital beds and using those beds  better so fundamentally that's the big  problem that requires investment that  means also it's not just beds but it's  the workforce to look after them and we  do need to expand our Workforce  um you know and that's right across the  patch  um the government did announce more  money for the NHS in November Crawford  was talking about that an extra 3.3  billion pounds in each of the next two  years and I guess some would argue that  you know it's not just about money it's  not just about putting more and more  money into the NHS it's about reform too  otherwise the money effectively is  wasted do you think that's true I think  that's fair I mean I I you know the NHS  is an enormous organization and there's  always things that we can do differently  and think innovatively about how we do  this and I think that's a fair challenge  that we need we need to do it but  repairing something that's broken cost a  lot of money  so more money is part of the issue that  reformed to yes but you're dealing with  something that's fundamentally just very  very fragile and broken and that takes a  lot of work and you know  short-term big payments to try and buy  um uh aren't the way to do it we  actually need sustained predictable  funding so we can use that well rather  than just and that means things like  Capital funding and actually building  capacity not just on short-term salary  fixes and just looking forward I know  you don't want to talk directly about  strikes which I understand there are  meetings going on uh tomorrow and our  strikes also having an impact on you  know the safe running of the NHS well we  need to have a sense of perspective you  know we've been in trouble for the last  for the last year we had three strike  those days in December we're only having  four strike days yes they're disruptive  yes  um they're they're difficult but you've  got a much bigger picture of terrible  working conditions that have been going  on before

